text:
The overwhelming number of white Chevrolets (a Malibu, a Damas minivan, Nexias and Cobalts) is a hallmark of Uzbekistan, where GM Uzbekistan dominates the car market. The wide Soviet-era boulevard, the cream-coloured Stalinist-style apartment block with ground-floor shops, the gantry-mounted traffic lights and the tree-lined median all point to a large post-Soviet capital. A Cyrillic-style shop sign and a large billboard fit Tashkent, and the scale and density suggest the city centre.

>>>
{"country":"Uzbekistan","place":"Tashkent","lat":41.3050,"lng":69.2700}

A courtyard car park ringed by mismatched pre-war apartment buildings, terracotta-tiled low houses and a newer glass tower suggests a Balkan capital's inner city. The ageing German cars (VW T4, Golf V, Mercedes W211, Audi A4) are typical of the Bulgarian fleet. The EU plates with blue strips fit too, and the scruffy whitewashed walls point strongly to central Sofia.

>>>
{"country":"Bulgaria","place":"Sofia","lat":42.6965,"lng":23.3290}

Sandstone tenements with bay windows, a Co-op "Funeralcare" shopfront and a police car in Battenberg livery point clearly to a Scottish city. The craggy hill at the end of the street looks like Arthur's Seat or Salisbury Crags, which puts this in Edinburgh. The tenement streets suggest the Easter Road / Leith Walk side of the city, looking south toward the hill, though the exact street is a guess.

>>>
{"country":"United Kingdom","place":"Easter Road, Edinburgh, Scotland","lat":55.9600,"lng":-3.1715}

Steep brown hillsides covered densely in unfinished brick houses, a deep high-altitude valley, clear Andean light and sparse dry-season trees all strongly point to La Paz, Bolivia. The large paved civic plaza with tall flagpoles and railings above lower ground suggests a central spot, perhaps near the Parque Urbano Central and Miraflores area.

>>>
{"country":"Bolivia","place":"La Paz","lat":-16.5025,"lng":-68.1250}

The sign reads "China Children's Store" (中国儿童用品商店), the well-known children's department store on Wangfujing pedestrian street. The red Spring Festival column banners say "京彩", a pun on Beijing, and the store sits beside luxury brand boutiques like Longines, which fits Wangfujing's main shopping strip in central Beijing.

>>>
{"country":"China","place":"Wangfujing Street, Dongcheng, Beijing","lat":39.9146,"lng":116.4107}

The wide granite-paved promenade, the classical palaces and the socialist-realist arcaded building on the right all point to central Warsaw. The statue set among flowerbeds on the left fits the Copernicus Monument in front of Staszic Palace. The green-domed church tower ahead would then be Holy Cross Church. That places the view at the junction of Nowy Świat and Krakowskie Przedmieście, looking north.

>>> 
{"country":"Poland","place":"Warsaw, Krakowskie Przedmieście near Copernicus Monument","lat":52.2382,"lng":21.0187}

The German traffic signals, the blue shared cycle-path sign, the bike lanes and the red sandstone bridge parapets all point to Germany. The cluster of high-rises ahead is clearly Frankfurt am Main's banking district. The view runs straight along a Main bridge toward the towers, which fits the Untermainbrücke seen from the Sachsenhausen (south) end.

>>>
{"country":"Germany","place":"Frankfurt am Main, Untermainbrücke (Sachsenhausen side)","lat":50.1046,"lng":8.6752}

The neoclassical white monumental building with pilasters and stripped classical cornices looks like interwar or early communist Eastern European architecture. The car mix, with an Opel Astra estate, a BMW 5 Series and an older Mercedes E-Class, together with the park-like setting and the lamp posts, fits Bucharest well. The likeliest spot is a building near the parks along Șoseaua Kiseleff in the north of the city.

>>>
{"country":"Romania","place":"Bucharest, Șoseaua Kiseleff","lat":44.4555,"lng":26.0842}

Rough, multicoloured fieldstone cobbles and red-brick buildings with German-style windows, seen in the reflection, point to a former Prussian or Hanseatic Baltic town. The EU plates and the mix of cars (Volvo, Lexus, Subaru) under a grey, wet sky suggest the Baltic states. Old Town Klaipėda in Lithuania, formerly Memel, fits this brick-and-cobble streetscape well.

>>>
{"country":"Lithuania","place":"Klaipėda Old Town","lat":55.7095,"lng":21.1335}

The car carries a French phone number starting 05 62, which points to southwest France: Hautes-Pyrénées, Gers or the Toulouse area. The French no-entry sign, yellow curb paint and Renault Clio support France. The slate dormer roofs, half-timbered house and red-and-white painted corner quoins fit Bigorre-style houses around Tarbes.

>>>
{"country":"France","place":"Tarbes, Hautes-Pyrénées","lat":43.2328,"lng":0.0781}

The station canopies, the trenitalia departure board with an Italian clock (showing a train to "Ancona"), and the overhead catenary all point to an Italian railway station. The modernist stone-clad pillars and the long flat concrete canopy match Firenze Santa Maria Novella. The scooters parked along the side platform fit the Via Alamanni side of that station.

>>>
{"country":"Italy","place":"Firenze Santa Maria Novella station, Florence","lat":43.7768,"lng":11.2472}

Traffic drives on the right, yet the vehicles look like older Japanese right-hand-drive imports, such as the white Toyota sedans and Suzuki vans. That mismatch is a signature of Myanmar. The wide concrete road, low white concrete median with striped bollards, and lush tropical greenery point to suburban Yangon. The cluttered wooden and concrete utility poles and the mid-rise buildings fit an arterial road in the northern townships of Yangon.

>>>
{"country":"Myanmar","place":"Yangon","lat":16.8350,"lng":96.1350}

The plain concrete road with saw-cut joint lines, the tin-roofed sari-sari store covered in Coca-Cola and Pepsi signs and printed tarpaulins, and the motorcycle parked out front are all typical of the rural Philippines. The coconut palms, banana plants and dense lowland vegetation suggest a humid lowland area in the Visayas or Mindanao. The modest provincial-road feel led me to commit to a Visayan island such as Bohol.

>>> 
{"country":"Philippines","place":"Bohol, Central Visayas","lat":9.85,"lng":124.15}

The pink-flowering lapacho (tajy) tree, the white Mercosur-style plates on a Kia Cerato and an older Nissan, and the many motorbikes all point to Paraguay. The concrete utility poles with dense tangles of overhead wires, the orange clay-tile roofs and the whitewashed walls fit that too. The flat, leafy urban grid looks like a residential-commercial street in Asunción.

>>>
{"country":"Paraguay","place":"Asunción","lat":-25.2950,"lng":-57.6100}

The white Fiat Egea, Renault Clio and Citroën C5 Aircross, together with the makeshift steel-canopied open-air car park and the site container, strongly suggest Turkey. Tall slender residential and hotel towers mixed with older mid-rise blocks, a flat coastal setting and a warm, hazy evening light fit a Mediterranean Turkish city. Mersin is the strongest match, since it is known for clusters of high-rise towers like these in its centre and Yenişehir area.

>>>
{"country":"Turkey","place":"Mersin","lat":36.7930,"lng":34.6150}

The "ATOCHA" lettering, Rafael Moneo's brick clock tower and cylindrical rotunda, and the ornate red-brick 19th-century iron-roofed station hall make this Madrid's Atocha station, currently under construction for the new through-station works. The viewpoint is next to the old station's façade near Glorieta de Carlos V, looking toward the Moneo vestibule.

>>>
{"country":"Spain","place":"Madrid, Atocha station","lat":40.4068,"lng":-3.6905}

The shop sign reads "Kedai Cenderahati I ♥ KL Gifts Malaysia" and carries a DBKL (Kuala Lumpur City Hall) licence number, which places it firmly in Kuala Lumpur. Malaysian flag bunting, women in hijab and the busy tourist pedestrian street suggest the Bukit Bintang shopping district. The souvenir and food stalls fit the stretch around the Jalan Alor end of Jalan Bukit Bintang.

>>>
{"country":"Malaysia","place":"Bukit Bintang, Kuala Lumpur","lat":3.1462,"lng":101.7088}

The paralelepípedo cobblestone paving, granite stone retaining walls, lush Atlantic-forest vegetation and old villas with balustrades and tiled gate roofs strongly suggest Brazil. The Mercedes Sprinter van and the sloping hillside residential lane fit Rio de Janeiro's older hill neighbourhoods. The mix of cobbles, overhanging trees and colonial-era homes points most specifically to Santa Teresa.

>>> 
{"country":"Brazil","place":"Santa Teresa, Rio de Janeiro","lat":-22.9215,"lng":-43.1885}

The "Made in Jamaica" billboard, left-hand traffic and the black-and-yellow painted kerbs on the median point strongly to Jamaica. The wide dual carriageway, dry scrubby grass and newer housing to the right fit the dry southern plains around Kingston. That suggests a road near Portmore or St. Catherine.

>>>
{"country":"Jamaica","place":"Portmore, St. Catherine","lat":17.9620,"lng":-76.8750}

The red corrugated-iron cladding, the Icelandic sheep in the large black-and-white photo, and the paved pedestrian street are typical of downtown Reykjavík. The shop sign "FOTOGRAFI" matches the well-known Fotografi photo gallery on Skólavörðustígur. Its window is full of old cameras and prints, and the Laugavegur area is close by.

>>>
{"country":"Iceland","place":"Skólavörðustígur, Reykjavík","lat":64.1461,"lng":-21.9312}

Left-hand traffic, red Bajaj tuk-tuks, Sinhala script on the billboard on the left and a Texstretch advert all point to Sri Lanka. The wide, divided urban road with metal median barriers, the mushroom-shaped concrete water tower and the nearby mosque minarets suggest an arterial road in Colombo. I can't pin the exact spot from the scene; Baseline Road around Dematagoda is my best guess.

>>>
{"country":"Sri Lanka","place":"Colombo, Baseline Road area","lat":6.9350,"lng":79.8770}

Soviet-era nine-story housing blocks in a mix of brick and concrete panels, together with European-style blue lane-direction signs and yellow diamond priority-road signs, point to the Baltics. The wide intersection, the separate red-surfaced bike paths and the style of the traffic lights fit Lithuania best. The brick-faced panel blocks look most like the large residential districts of Klaipėda, such as those along Taikos prospektas, though that city-level call is the least certain part.

>>>
{"country":"Lithuania","place":"Klaipėda","lat":55.6905,"lng":21.1690}

The yellow dashed lane line is a Swiss bike-lane marking. Overhead tram wires and a tram shelter with poster boards also point to Switzerland. The grand sandstone neoclassical building beside a modern corten-clad block suggests Zurich's university quarter around ETH, likely Universitätstrasse or Tannenstrasse, though that exact spot is a best guess.

>>>
{"country":"Switzerland","place":"Zurich, Universitätstrasse (ETH area)","lat":47.3782,"lng":8.5480}

The billboard text looks Hungarian, and the traffic arrangement shows right-hand driving. The blue European lane-direction sign, the old yellow railway-style building with many chimneys, the tram-era wide roads and the wooded hills in the distance all point to the Buda side of Budapest. The setting most plausibly matches the Óbuda/Bécsi út area.

>>> 
{"country":"Hungary","place":"Budapest, Óbuda","lat":47.5480,"lng":19.0230}

The overhead gantry of black matrix boxes showing 70 km/h, together with the white dashed lane lines, multi-lane urban motorway, overpasses and tall lamp posts, matches Belgium's motorway traffic-management systems. Ringways around Antwerp (the R1) commonly use 70 km/h gantries like this. The flat, green, overcast setting and the heavy truck traffic also fit the Antwerp ring.

>>>
{"country":"Belgium","place":"Antwerp, R1 Ring","lat":51.1960,"lng":4.4290}

The grey limestone rubble walls patched with red brick are classic northern Baltic construction, and Tallinn's limestone is the strongest single clue. The standing-seam metal roofs, horse chestnut trees, gravel lot left vacant, heavy graffiti and modern white office blocks next to old walls all fit central Tallinn. My best guess is the city centre around the Maakri, Kentmanni and Rotermann quarters.

>>>
{"country":"Estonia","place":"Tallinn","lat":59.4325,"lng":24.7560}

The dark timber and brick facades, the gravel car park, the birch trees and the parked Volvo S60 and other European-plated cars all point to Scandinavia. The round no-parking sign and the large institutional building with a curved metal roof, likely a school or hospital campus, fit Sweden best. The early autumn colours and sparse vegetation suggest the north, so I'm placing it in a town in inland Norrland, though the specific town is a low-confidence guess.

>>>
{"country":"Sweden","place":"Lycksele, Västerbotten","lat":64.5955,"lng":18.6735}

The Georgian Mkhedruli script on the car wash sign ("self-service" plus "Bubble Wash") clearly places this in Georgia. The flat terrain, lush green trees, bright summer sky and unfinished concrete block building fit a lowland western Georgian city. That points toward the Black Sea coast, with Batumi as the most likely candidate.

>>>
{"country":"Georgia","place":"Batumi","lat":41.6280,"lng":41.6250}

The buildings are faced in grayish-brown volcanic tuff and basalt, with Soviet-era balconies and air conditioners, which is classic for Yerevan. The modern rounded glass tower, the newer cars (a Porsche Cayenne among them) and the paved promenade beside a low wall and a planted drop all point to central Yerevan. The drop on the right may be a small gorge, possibly the Getar river channel, though that part is less certain.

>>>
{"country":"Armenia","place":"Yerevan","lat":40.1790,"lng":44.5180}

Traditional Chinese signage, green bilingual street-name signs, scooter-lined arcade sidewalks and the 美而美 breakfast chain point clearly to Taiwan. The street signs read "Rehe" (熱河) and 青島街 (Qingdao St), and the shop is labelled 熱河店 (Rehe branch). A Rehe Road and a Qingdao Road sit near each other in Taichung's North District, so I'm placing it there, though I'm not certain the two streets intersect.

>>>
{"country":"Taiwan","place":"North District, Taichung","lat":24.1625,"lng":120.6875}

The tram wires, the Austrian traffic light and the white district-number street sign ("3., ...") point to Vienna's Landstraße district. The green "CAT City Airport Train – 16 min to airport – City Check-in" signage is unique to the Wien Mitte transport hub. The building marked "...zentrum Wien Mitte" on the right fits the Justizzentrum on Marxergasse, beside the glass towers of the Wien Mitte complex.

>>>
{"country":"Austria","place":"Vienna, Wien Mitte (Landstraße)","lat":48.2062,"lng":16.3858}

This is Japan: traffic drives on the left, the white minivan is a Toyota Noah/Voxy, and the elevated urban expressway has the dashed white lane markings and concrete parapets typical of Japanese city highways. The tall clear noise barriers topped with cylindrical noise-reducer tubes are common on the urban expressway networks of the Nagoya and Hanshin regions. The mid-rise apartment blocks, transmission pylons and generally flat terrain point to a Nagoya-area expressway, though Hanshin remains possible.

>>>
{"country":"Japan","place":"Nagoya, Aichi","lat":35.2100,"lng":136.9300}

The cobbled Scandinavian harbourfront, the moored historic sailing ships, the corten-steel public sculpture, the low sun of a northern summer evening and the shared city bike all point to Gothenburg, Sweden. The quayside with red-pillared modern architecture and the view across the Göta älv fits the Lilla Bommen / Göteborgsoperan waterfront area. That identification is a best match rather than a confirmed landmark.

>>>
{"country":"Sweden","place":"Gothenburg, Lilla Bommen","lat":57.7105,"lng":11.9665}

Greek-script signage ("ΕΜΠΟΡΙΑ", "Σταύρος Στ. Μαρμαρ...") and the yellow Euronet-style ATM point clearly to Greece. The narrow pedestrian-ish lane, wholesale shoe shop, heavy graffiti, metal shutters and mid-century concrete apartment blocks with split AC units are classic central Athens. That mix fits the Psyrri/Monastiraki commercial district near Athinas and Ermou streets.

>>>
{"country":"Greece","place":"Athens, Psyrri/Monastiraki","lat":37.9785,"lng":23.7250}

The billboard is in Malagasy ("Ianao no boss", "1 TAona") and mentions BNI Madagascar, so this is Madagascar. Traffic drives on the right, and the old Renault 4 and the many scooters fit the capital. The wide boulevard with flag rows, car dealerships and modern office blocks suggests Antananarivo's commercial district, likely around Ankorondrano or the Route des Hydrocarbures.

>>>
{"country":"Madagascar","place":"Ankorondrano, Antananarivo","lat":-18.8850,"lng":47.5220}

Socialist-era mid-rise apartment blocks with peeling pastel and red facades, wall-mounted AC units and wide grass verges strongly suggest the former Yugoslavia. The "no stopping" sign has a red supplementary towing plate beneath it, which is typical of Serbian cities. The flat terrain and the elevated structure in the distance point to Novi Sad, likely one of its residential estates such as Liman.

>>>
{"country":"Serbia","place":"Novi Sad","lat":45.2465,"lng":19.8420}

This is a public granite drinking fountain (česma), a very typical feature of Bosnia and Herzegovina, and it carries a municipal coat of arms with a stylised skyline of buildings around a central tower. The hillside behind is densely covered with red-tiled houses and some apartment blocks among green deciduous and conifer trees, which fits a Sarajevo valley setting. The modern skyline emblem suggests one of Sarajevo's municipalities, possibly Novi Grad or Novo Sarajevo, though I can't identify the crest with certainty.

>>>
{"country":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","place":"Sarajevo","lat":43.8516,"lng":18.3866}

Yellow ribbons tied along the fence are a strong sign of Israel, where they mark the hostage campaign that began in late 2023, and there is faint Hebrew lettering on the white building. The shaded playground with a fabric canopy sits in a tree-lined median between traffic lanes, with ficus trees, white modernist apartment blocks and a city bus. That layout is typical of Tel Aviv's central boulevards, such as Ben Gurion or Chen.

>>> 
{"country":"Israel","place":"Tel Aviv","lat":32.0835,"lng":34.7765}

The yellow dashed centre line on a separated cycle/pedestrian path is distinctly Norwegian, and Scandinavian-style chain-link fencing with granite kerbs fits too. The electrified railway with a freight/container area on the right and modern office blocks suggest a city rail corridor. That points to the Bergen line near the Nygårdstangen–Kronstad area in Bergen.

>>>
{"country":"Norway","place":"Bergen, Kronstad/Fløen","lat":60.3800,"lng":5.3430}

The street sign reads "Boulevard Aliou Ardo Sow", and the "Mega Teral" billboard uses Wolof wording, which points strongly to Senegal. The dry sandy verges, oleander bushes, mid-rise concrete buildings, right-hand traffic and dusty harmattan haze all fit Dakar. I believe this boulevard is in the newer central districts near the VDN, around Sacré-Cœur and Keur Gorgui, but I'm less sure of that exact placement.

>>> 
{"country":"Senegal","place":"Dakar, Sacré-Cœur / Keur Gorgui","lat":14.7155,"lng":-17.4650}

Grass-overgrown tram tracks on what looks like a turning loop, concrete catenary poles, a red brick fence with capped pillars, and stadium floodlights behind a sports fence all point strongly to a post-Soviet city, most likely in Ukraine. The parked European cars and lush deciduous trees fit a large Ukrainian city with an extensive old tram network. Kharkiv is my best guess, near one of its stadium areas, though the exact spot is uncertain.

>>>
{"country":"Ukraine","place":"Kharkiv","lat":49.9810,"lng":36.2610}

This is a manicured tropical landscape with a Canary Island date palm, young bottle palms and Christmas palms. Clean painted concrete curbs and a smooth private-looking road suggest a gated resort or botanical garden in the Caribbean. The lush lawn, dry-season light and resort-style planting fit the Dominican Republic well, for example the Casa de Campo resort in La Romana, though this is an educated guess rather than a confirmed match.

>>>
{"country":"Dominican Republic","place":"Casa de Campo, La Romana","lat":18.4140,"lng":-68.9200}

Red taxis, left-hand traffic, dense clusters of very tall residential towers, and Hong Kong-style road markings and barriers make this clearly Hong Kong. The wide expressway lined with roadwork barriers and newer high-rise estates suggests West Kowloon, probably the Lai Chi Kok / Cheung Sha Wan stretch along the West Kowloon Highway.

>>>
{"country":"Hong Kong","place":"Lai Chi Kok / Cheung Sha Wan, Kowloon","lat":22.3345,"lng":114.1505}

Dutch brick-paved (klinker) roads, white dashed edge markings, a "zone 30 end" sign above a yield triangle, dark green bollards and a cyclist all point strongly to the Netherlands. The wooded setting with mature trees and older villa-style houses with dark tiled roofs suggests an affluent leafy suburb, like those around Haarlem, Bloemendaal or Heemstede. Nothing in the photo pins down the exact town, so my point is a best guess within that area.

>>>
{"country":"Netherlands","place":"Haarlem / Bloemendaal","lat":52.3900,"lng":4.6250}

Right-hand traffic, black-and-yellow kerbs, black-and-white striped sign posts, manicured date-palm medians and arid haze all point to the Gulf. The beige electrical substation kiosk and the yellowish rear plate style are typical of Oman. Wide landscaped boulevards with an overpass in the distance suggest the Muscat urban area, likely around Al Khuwair or Al Khoudh.

>>>
{"country":"Oman","place":"Muscat, Al Khuwair area","lat":23.5880,"lng":58.4100}

The overhead sign reads "Av. La Cultura, Urb. La Campiña, Calle Bolívar", which is Spanish. The cars carry Peruvian-style plates with reflective strips. The intense high-altitude sunlight and clear sky, together with the half-finished red-brick buildings, point to an Andean city in Peru. Avenida de la Cultura is Cusco's main arterial road, so I place this in the eastern Wanchaq–San Sebastián area of Cusco.

>>>
{"country":"Peru","place":"Cusco, Av. de la Cultura","lat":-13.5280,"lng":-71.9400}

This is traditional Korean palace architecture: giwa roof tiles with white-mortared ends, red-painted rafters with white circular ends, and grey brick walls. The green plaque carries Hanja characters that appear to begin with "景" followed by a character with the 示 radical, likely "福". That points to a Joseon royal palace in Seoul, most plausibly Gyeongbokgung.

>>>
{"country":"South Korea","place":"Gyeongbokgung, Seoul","lat":37.5796,"lng":126.9770}

Left-hand traffic, the red-and-yellow chevron rear markings on the truck, the precast concrete palisade walls and the Jersey barriers all point strongly to South Africa. The lush subtropical greenery, Norfolk Island pines and humid, cloudy coastal sky fit KwaZulu-Natal, most likely a freeway through the Durban metro area.

>>> 
{"country":"South Africa","place":"Durban, KwaZulu-Natal","lat":-29.8250,"lng":30.9900}

The mid-century ochre and white modernist buildings, tall Washingtonia palms, the overcast highland sky and the lush green lawn point to East Africa, most likely Nairobi. The carved, curved abstract sculpture on a campus lawn, with downtown towers behind, fits the University of Nairobi main campus near University Way, at the edge of the CBD.

>>>
{"country":"Kenya","place":"University of Nairobi, Nairobi","lat":-1.2797,"lng":36.8160}

The sign "8 tímar 9-18" uses the Faroese/Icelandic word for hours, and the blue parking sign design matches the Faroe Islands. The treeless green hills, mossy rock outcrop, overcast sky and tall stadium floodlight masts fit the Gundadalur sports area in Tórshavn, near Tórsvøllur stadium. A Scania vehicle dealership and the industrial sports hall nearby are consistent with the edge of the capital.

>>>
{"country":"Faroe Islands","place":"Tórshavn, Gundadalur","lat":62.0185,"lng":-6.7835}

The Portuguese calçada mosaic paving, wrought-iron balconies, tiled façades and Pombaline building blocks point to Lisbon. The triumphal arch at the end of the pedestrian street is the Arco da Rua Augusta, so this is Rua Augusta in the Baixa, looking south toward Praça do Comércio. House number 251 suggests the northern end of the street, near Rossio.

>>> 
{"country":"Portugal","place":"Rua Augusta, Baixa, Lisbon","lat":38.7118,"lng":-9.1384}

The tall, slim cylindrical glass tower is unmistakably the Obel Tower in Belfast. Cars drive on the left, there are UK-style yellow rear number plates and an NCP multi-storey car park, and the road is a grade-separated urban motorway with overhead gantries. Together these point to the M3/Westlink link near the York Street interchange, looking toward Donegall Quay.

>>>
{"country":"United Kingdom","place":"Belfast, Northern Ireland","lat":54.6062,"lng":-5.9255}

The hotel sign reads "Lindner Hotel City Plaza", which is in Cologne, Germany. That fits the German road signs and the right-hand traffic. The red-brick hotel facade, the modern glass offices and the red double-decker sightseeing bus point to Magnusstraße, near Friesenstraße and the Gereonsviertel in central Cologne.

>>>
{"country":"Germany","place":"Cologne, Magnusstraße","lat":50.9409,"lng":6.9452}

Red-and-white plastic road barriers, oleander hedges and the European motorhomes (a Sunlight camper) point to northern Italy. The wide, flat lagoon with a small wooded island and a low distant skyline looks like the Venetian Lagoon. A large camper parking lot right on the lagoon edge fits the Fusina terminal area near Venice, across from islets like San Giorgio in Alga.

>>>
{"country":"Italy","place":"Fusina, Venice","lat":45.4185,"lng":12.2575}

The tall eucalyptus trees, the dry Mediterranean-looking grass, left-hand-drive-side parking and Australian-style 20 km/h speed signs point to Australia, as do the Toyota HiAce and Ford Territory. The white number plate with dark characters fits South Australia. The gravel car park under gums, the 20 km/h limit and the red-brick buildings suggest a university campus in Adelaide; I'm guessing the Waite campus at Urrbrae, though Flinders University is a close alternative.

>>>
{"country":"Australia","place":"Adelaide, South Australia","lat":-34.9670,"lng":138.6360}

The green "WOLNE" sign, meaning free spaces, on the car-park display and the "UWAGA" warning sign on the hoarding are Polish. The paving-brick sidewalk, the old brick tenement with firewall chimneys and the Polish-style P parking signage fit a central Polish city. The mix of 19th-century tenements, infill construction and paid surface car parks feels like Kraków, perhaps around Kazimierz or Grzegórzki, but that city-level guess is an impression rather than something a sign confirms.

>>>
{"country":"Poland","place":"Kraków","lat":50.0565,"lng":19.9505}

The Spanish signage ("Promoción", "Copias"), the price "4.995" in a thousands format typical of Costa Rican colones, and the MST phone-accessory chain all point to Costa Rica. The wide brick-paved pedestrian boulevard with ornate lampposts, crowds of pigeons, a gazebo-style kiosk and bronze figure statues fits San José's Avenida Central bulevar. This spot looks like the stretch beside the Banco Central plaza, which is known for its bronze statues.

>>>
{"country":"Costa Rica","place":"San José, Avenida Central","lat":9.9334,"lng":-84.0786}

Red brick townhouses with pale stone trim, white louvred shutters and an Art Nouveau stone facade are typical of Picardy and Hauts-de-France. The French road markings with dashed lane lines and cycle symbols, plus the European plates, confirm France. The wide straight 19th-century street with this brick-and-stone style strongly suggests Amiens, likely the Henriville area or the city centre.

>>>
{"country":"France","place":"Amiens, Hauts-de-France","lat":49.8885,"lng":2.2985}

The banner reading "Philippine Student Quill Merit Awardees" and the tropical vegetation point to the Philippines. The massive old stone fortification walls on the right are characteristic of Intramuros in Manila. A large institutional building running along the inner walls, with a bike lane and bollards, fits Muralla Street near Mapúa University.

>>>
{"country":"Philippines","place":"Intramuros, Manila","lat":14.5903,"lng":120.9772}

The green sign is in Nastaliq Urdu script. A man in shalwar kameez, a white Suzuki-style pickup and traffic on the left all point strongly to Pakistan. The dense, dusty mid-rise concrete apartment blocks with ground-floor shutter shops, the tangle of overhead wires and the dry coastal haze are typical of Karachi's older inner neighbourhoods, such as Lyari, Liaquatabad or Nazimabad.

>>> 
{"country":"Pakistan","place":"Karachi, Sindh","lat":24.8800,"lng":67.0150}

The continuous row of white 1960s–70s polykatoikia apartment blocks with deep balconies and awnings, facing a wide stone promenade with green iron railings and mooring bollards, is unmistakably Leoforos Nikis in Thessaloniki. The sea is on the right and the low coastline of Kalamaria curves away in the distance, which puts the view looking southeast along the waterfront between Aristotelous Square and the White Tower. The Lime scooter and the Greek-style seafront layout fit this too.

>>>
